SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) system designed for a multi-user environment. It supports complex role management features including role hierarchy, temporary delegation of roles, revocation of delegated roles, and concurrent request processing. Users and system objects are each assigned security clearance or classification levels (High, Medium, Low). Authorization correctness and permission consistency must be preserved while supporting concurrent requests processed in FIFO order, with guaranteed liveness. Higher-clearance users can approve or reject requests from lower-clearance users and delegate roles temporarily under clearance constraints. The system enforces strict clearance-based access control and manages delegation expiration and revocation.

An Emergency Module is to be integrated as a new component. This module introduces a special Emergency mode triggered by exactly two administrators simultaneously, during which certain operational behaviors and privileges change temporarily. After the emergency ends, the system must revert to Normal mode, revoke emergency privileges and delegated roles issued during the emergency, and require administrators to update their credentials immediately before any further operation.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- Users have assigned security clearance levels: High, Medium, or Low.
- Administrators are users with High clearance, but not all High-clearance users are administrators.
- Users hold assigned or delegated roles, subject to clearance constraints.
- System objects have classification levels: High, Medium, or Low.
- Requests operate on one or more objects and have classification equal to the highest classification of affected objects.
- Users can only submit requests with classification less than or equal to their clearance.
- Higher-clearance users can approve or reject requests from lower-clearance users.
- Requests are processed concurrently but in FIFO order during Normal mode.
- Every submitted request is eventually processed (liveness).
- Higher-clearance users can temporarily delegate assigned roles to lower-clearance users if the delegate meets the role’s minimum clearance.
- Delegated roles expire after a fixed period or can be revoked anytime by administrators.
- Delegation duration and expiry are abstracted; no detailed temporal delegation expiry constraints are modeled.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Mode Triggering  
- While in Normal mode, exactly two administrators must jointly trigger an emergency simultaneously to cause the system to enter Emergency mode.  
- This triggering is modeled abstractly as a simultaneous event by a set of exactly two administrators, without detailed concurrency semantics.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration and Recovery  
- Emergency mode remains active only for a limited, finite time.  
- The system must eventually return to Normal mode after the emergency duration.

R3: Emergency Mode Operations  
- R3.1: Request processing may bypass FIFO ordering to allow prioritization and concurrent processing of multiple high-classification requests.  
- R3.2: Users may temporarily hold mutually exclusive roles during Emergency mode (contrary to normal exclusivity constraints).  
- R3.3: Administrators temporarily hold emergency privileges during Emergency mode, which are an abstract set of permissions separate from and do not override existing administrator privileges.  
- R3.4: Requests submitted exactly at the Normal-to-Emergency mode boundary must be held until Emergency mode is fully active, then processed according to Emergency mode priority: all High-classification requests first, then Medium, then Low.

R4: Post-Emergency Cleanup and Updates  
- R4.1: Emergency privileges granted during Emergency mode must be revoked when Emergency mode ends.  
- R4.2: Roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked at the end of the emergency; delegated roles assigned before Emergency mode continue as usual and may be expired or revoked by administrators during Emergency mode.  
- R4.3: Administrators must update their credentials immediately after Emergency mode ends and before any further system operation. This is a soft constraint; the system does not enforce blocking, but administrators must comply before further operations.

R5: Modeling and Verification Constraints (New)  
- R5.1: All constraints and facts must be guarded by the current system mode (Normal or Emergency) to avoid contradictory or vacuous conditions, e.g., mutual exclusivity constraints apply only in Normal mode.  
- R5.2: Syntax and modeling must respect Alloy’s relational projection rules and abstraction principles to ensure successful verification.  
- R5.3: Temporal aspects such as delegation duration and Emergency mode duration are modeled abstractly; no detailed temporal constraints are enforced.  
- R5.4: Credential updates after Emergency mode are modeled as soft constraints and not enforced by system blocking but verified by assertions or test predicates.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: An individual operating in the system, characterized by a security clearance level.  
- Administrator: A user with High clearance and special administrative privileges.  
- Security Clearance Level: Classification of users (High, Medium, Low).  
- Role: A set of permissions assigned to users; some roles may be mutually exclusive.  
- Delegated Role: A role temporarily assigned to a user by a higher-clearance user.  
- System Object: An entity affected by user requests, having a classification level.  
- Request: An action submitted by a user affecting one or more system objects; associated with a classification level.  
- Emergency Mode: A special system operational state triggered by exactly two administrators simultaneously, altering request processing and role constraints.  
- Emergency Privileges: Temporary privileges granted to administrators during Emergency mode; these privileges are separate from existing administrator privileges and do not override them.  
- Normal Mode: The default system operational state.  
- FIFO Order: First-In-First-Out order of request processing under normal operation.  
- Mutual Exclusion of Roles: Constraint preventing certain roles from being held by the same user simultaneously under normal operation; this constraint is relaxed only during Emergency mode.

ASSUMPTIONS REQUIRING CLARIFICATION
====================================
1. Does "exactly 2 administrators" triggering Emergency mode require both administrators to trigger simultaneously, or is it sufficient that two distinct administrators have triggered it within some timeframe or sequence?  
