**Current Requirements:**
```
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) system designed for a multi-user environment. It supports complex role management features including role hierarchy, temporary delegation of roles, revocation of delegated roles, and concurrent request processing. Users and system objects are each assigned security clearance or classification levels (High, Medium, Low). Authorization correctness and permission consistency must be preserved while supporting concurrent requests processed in FIFO order, with guaranteed liveness. Higher-clearance users can approve or reject requests from lower-clearance users and delegate roles temporarily under clearance constraints. The system enforces strict clearance-based access control and manages delegation expiration and revocation.

An Emergency Module is to be integrated as a new component. This module introduces a special Emergency mode triggered by exactly two administrators simultaneously, during which certain operational behaviors and privileges change temporarily. After the emergency ends, the system must revert to Normal mode, revoke emergency privileges and delegated roles issued during the emergency, and require administrators to update their credentials immediately before any further operation.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- Users have assigned security clearance levels: High, Medium, or Low.
- Administrators are users with High clearance, but not all High-clearance users are administrators.
- Users hold assigned or delegated roles, subject to clearance constraints.
- System objects have classification levels: High, Medium, or Low.
- Requests operate on one or more objects and have classification equal to the highest classification of affected objects.
- Users can only submit requests with classification less than or equal to their clearance.
- Higher-clearance users can approve or reject requests from lower-clearance users.
- Requests are processed concurrently but in FIFO order during Normal mode.
- Every submitted request is eventually processed (liveness).
- Higher-clearance users can temporarily delegate assigned roles to lower-clearance users if the delegate meets the role’s minimum clearance.
- Delegated roles expire after a fixed period or can be revoked anytime by administrators.
- Delegation duration and expiry are abstracted; no detailed temporal delegation expiry constraints are modeled.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Mode Triggering  
- While in Normal mode, exactly two administrators must jointly trigger an emergency simultaneously to cause the system to enter Emergency mode.  
- This triggering is modeled abstractly as an atomic, simultaneous event by a set of exactly two administrators, explicitly documented to avoid misinterpretation that any sequence or timeframe suffices.  
- NOTE: The abstraction of simultaneity means both administrators trigger the emergency at the same system time point; no ordering or delay between triggers is permitted.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration and Recovery  
- Emergency mode remains active only for a limited, finite time modeled abstractly without specifying exact duration.  
- The system must eventually return to Normal mode after the emergency duration.

R3: Emergency Mode Operations  
- R3.1: Request processing may bypass FIFO ordering to allow prioritization and concurrent processing of multiple high-classification requests.  
- R3.2: Users may temporarily hold mutually exclusive roles during Emergency mode (contrary to normal exclusivity constraints).  
- R3.3: Administrators temporarily hold emergency privileges only during Emergency mode. These emergency privileges are an abstract set of permissions strictly additive to, and separate from, existing administrator privileges; they do not override or replace existing privileges.  
- R3.4: Requests submitted exactly at the Normal-to-Emergency mode boundary must be held until Emergency mode is fully active, then processed according to Emergency mode priority: all High-classification requests first, then Medium, then Low.

R4: Post-Emergency Cleanup and Updates  
- R4.1: Emergency privileges granted during Emergency mode must be revoked immediately when Emergency mode ends.  
- R4.2: Roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked at the end of the emergency; delegated roles assigned before Emergency mode continue as usual and may be expired or revoked by administrators during Emergency mode.  
- R4.3: Administrators must update their credentials immediately after Emergency mode ends and before any further system operation. This is a soft constraint; the system does not enforce blocking, but compliance is verified by assertions or test predicates.

R5: Modeling and Verification Constraints  
- R5.1: All constraints and facts must be explicitly guarded by the current system mode (Normal or Emergency) to prevent contradictory or vacuous conditions; for example, mutual exclusivity constraints apply only in Normal mode and are fully relaxed in Emergency mode.  
- R5.2: Syntax and modeling must respect Alloy’s relational projection rules and abstraction principles to ensure successful verification.  
- R5.3: Temporal aspects such as delegation duration and Emergency mode duration are modeled abstractly; no detailed temporal constraints are enforced.  
- R5.4: Credential updates after Emergency mode are modeled as soft constraints verified by assertions or test predicates; no system blocking is implemented.  
- R5.5: All conditional logic in Alloy models must be expressed using relational logic and guarded formulas compatible with Alloy syntax.  
- R5.6: Alloy models must place all `open` statements at the very beginning of the model file to comply with Alloy 6 syntax requirements.  
- R5.7: Comments and documentation in Alloy models must be placed in syntactically permitted locations to avoid parsing errors; adherence to Alloy 6 syntax for comments is mandatory.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: An individual operating in the system, characterized by a security clearance level.  
- Administrator: A user with High clearance and special administrative privileges.  
- Security Clearance Level: Classification of users (High, Medium, Low).  
- Role: A set of permissions assigned to users; some roles may be mutually exclusive.  
- Delegated Role: A role temporarily assigned to a user by a higher-clearance user.  
- System Object: An entity affected by user requests, having a classification level.  
- Request: An action submitted by a user affecting one or more system objects; associated with a classification level.  
- Emergency Mode: A special system operational state triggered by exactly two administrators simultaneously, altering request processing and role constraints.  
- Emergency Privileges: Temporary privileges granted to administrators only during Emergency mode; these privileges are an abstract set of permissions strictly additive to existing administrator privileges and do not override or replace them.  
- Normal Mode: The default system operational state.  
- FIFO Order: First-In-First-Out order of request processing under normal operation.  
- Mutual Exclusion of Roles: Constraint preventing certain roles from being held by the same user simultaneously under normal operation; this constraint is fully relaxed only during Emergency mode.

ASSUMPTIONS REQUIRING CLARIFICATION
====================================
1. Does "exactly 2 administrators" triggering Emergency mode require both administrators to trigger simultaneously, or is it sufficient that two distinct administrators have triggered it within some timeframe or sequence?  
   - Clarification: Both administrators must trigger the emergency simultaneously to enter Emergency mode. This is modeled abstractly as an atomic simultaneous event by exactly two administrators at the same system time point.

2. What is the relationship between the emergency privileges and existing administrator privileges? Are emergency privileges additive, exclusive, or do they override existing privileges?  
   - Clarification: Emergency privileges are an abstract set of permissions strictly additive to and separate from existing administrator privileges; they do not override or replace existing privileges. These emergency privileges exist only during Emergency mode and are revoked immediately at its end.

3. Are mutual exclusive roles only relaxed during Emergency mode, or are there specific exceptions or limits to this relaxation?  
   - Clarification: Mutual exclusivity of roles is fully relaxed only during Emergency mode; normal exclusivity constraints apply otherwise.

4. After Emergency mode ends, must administrators update credentials immediately before any further operation, or is it sufficient that it happens eventually?  
   - Clarification: Administrators must update their credentials immediately after Emergency mode ends and before any further system operations. This is a soft constraint, not enforced by system blocking but verified by assertions or test predicates.

5. When revoking delegated roles after Emergency mode (R4.2), does this include all delegated roles active during the emergency, or only those delegated specifically during Emergency mode?  
   - Clarification: Only roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked at emergency end; delegated roles assigned prior to Emergency mode remain in effect and can be revoked or expired normally during Emergency mode.

6. How are requests submitted exactly at the Normal-to-Emergency boundary handled?  
   - Clarification: Such requests must be held until Emergency mode is fully active, then processed in priority order according to Emergency mode rules: High-classification requests first, then Medium, then Low.

CONSTRAINTS
===========
- Role delegation must respect clearance constraints at all times, except during Emergency mode when mutual exclusivity constraints are fully relaxed.  
- Requests submitted by users must have classification less than or equal to the user's clearance.  
- Requests are processed in FIFO order during Normal mode.  
- During Emergency mode, high-classification requests may be prioritized and processed concurrently, bypassing FIFO order.  
- Requests submitted exactly at the Normal-to-Emergency mode boundary are held until Emergency mode is fully active, then processed by priority: High first, Medium second, Low last.  
- Delegated roles expire after a fixed time or can be revoked anytime by administrators; delegation duration and expiry are abstracted without detailed temporal modeling.  
- Emergency mode can only be triggered by exactly two administrators simultaneously while in Normal mode; this triggering is modeled abstractly as an atomic simultaneous event by exactly two administrators at the same system time point.  
- Emergency mode duration is limited and modeled abstractly with a finite but unspecified length; the system must return to Normal mode eventually.  
- Emergency privileges and delegated roles granted during Emergency mode must be revoked immediately when Emergency mode ends; no additional explicit lifecycle modeling of emergency privileges beyond delegated role revocation is required.  
- Administrators must update their credentials immediately after Emergency mode ends and before any further operations; this is a soft constraint verified by assertions or test predicates and not enforced by system blocking.  
- All constraints, including mutual exclusivity, are explicitly guarded by the current system mode to prevent contradictory or vacuous conditions, ensuring normal constraints apply only in Normal mode and are relaxed in Emergency mode.  
- Modeling abstractions (simultaneous triggering, delegation duration, credential update enforcement) are explicitly documented to align verification expectations and system design.  
- All conditional logic in Alloy models must be expressed using relational logic and guarded formulas compatible with Alloy syntax.  
- Alloy model files must place all `open` statements at the very beginning to comply with Alloy 6 syntax.  
- Comments and documentation within Alloy models must adhere to Alloy 6 syntax and be placed in locations that do not cause parsing errors.
```

**[LESSON]:**  
Explicit guarding of constraints by system mode is crucial to avoid vacuous or contradictory verifications. User preferences favor abstract modeling of simultaneity and temporal constraints, with soft constraints verified by assertions providing a practical balance between enforcement and verification feasibility. Clear documentation of modeling abstractions and Alloy syntax requirements facilitates successful formal verification and team adherence.

**[EVENT]:**  
Verification feedback revealed ambiguities in simultaneous triggering, emergency privilege lifecycle, and mutual exclusivity relaxation. User clarifications confirmed that modeling simultaneous triggering as an atomic event by exactly two administrators at the same time is sufficient, emergency privileges are additive and temporary without override, and mutual exclusivity is fully relaxed only during Emergency mode. Requirements were updated to explicitly document these points, enforce mode-guarded constraints, clarify soft constraints, and add Alloy syntax compliance notes, thereby resolving ambiguities and aligning specifications with verification capabilities and user expectations.


**Current Alloy Model:**
```alloy
// Alloy 6 model of RBAC system with Emergency Module per updated requirements and feedback

open util/ordering[Time];

// === Signatures ===
// Time for temporal modeling
// SystemMode is either Normal or Emergency at each Time
abstract sig SystemMode {}
one sig Normal, Emergency extends SystemMode {}

// Security clearance levels
abstract sig ClearanceLevel {}
one sig High, Medium, Low extends ClearanceLevel {}

// User with clearance; some users are administrators
sig User {
  clearance: one ClearanceLevel,
  assignedRoles: set Role,            // assigned roles (non-delegated)
  delegatedRoles: set DelegatedRole  // delegated roles currently held
}
// Administrator: subset of users with High clearance and admin privilege
sig Administrator in User {}
fact AdminClearance { all a: Administrator | a.clearance = High }

// Role with minimum clearance and mutual exclusion constraints
sig Role {
  minClearance: one ClearanceLevel,
  mutuallyExclusive: set Role
}

// DelegatedRole links a Role to a User with delegation metadata
sig DelegatedRole {
  role: one Role,
  delegate: one User,
  delegatedBy: one User,       // delegator with higher clearance
  delegationTime: one Time,
  expiryTime: one Time,
  revoked: Bool               // use Alloy built-in Bool type for revoked flag
}

// SystemObject with classification
sig SystemObject {
  classification: one ClearanceLevel
}

// Request submitted by a user, operates on objects, with classification derived
sig Request {
  submitter: one User,
  objects: set SystemObject,
  classification: one ClearanceLevel,
  submittedAt: one Time,
  processedAt: lone Time       // time when processed; none if not yet processed
}

// Emergency Privileges: abstract permissions granted only in Emergency mode to admins
sig EmergencyPrivilege {}
// Emergency privileges granted to administrators during Emergency mode
sig EmergencyPrivilegeGrant {
  admin: one Administrator,
  privilege: one EmergencyPrivilege,
  grantedAt: one Time,
  revokedAt: lone Time
}

// System state at each Time: mode, requests queue, credential update state
sig SystemState {
  time: one Time,
  mode: one SystemMode,
  requests: set Request,
  // Credential update flags for administrators after Emergency mode ends
  credentialUpdated: User -> one Bool
}

// === Facts encoding existing system assumptions and constraints ===

// Clearance ordering helper predicate
pred clearanceAtLeast[c1, c2: ClearanceLevel] {
  (c1 = c2) or
  (c1 = High and c2 != High) or
  (c1 = Medium and c2 = Low)
}

// Administrator definition (subset of users with High clearance)
fact AdminDefinition {
  all a: Administrator | a in User and a.clearance = High
}

// Users hold roles subject to clearance constraints (except during Emergency mode)
fact UserRoleClearanceConstraint {
  all s: SystemState | all u: User | 
    (s.mode = Normal => (
      // Assigned roles min clearance <= user clearance
      all r: u.assignedRoles | clearanceAtLeast[u.clearance, r.minClearance] and
      // Delegated roles also respect clearance at delegation time outside Emergency mode
      all dr: u.delegatedRoles | clearanceAtLeast[u.clearance, dr.role.minClearance]
    )) or
    (s.mode = Emergency => true) // No clearance check enforced during Emergency mode here
}

// Mutual exclusion of roles enforced only in Normal mode
fact MutualExclusionNormalMode {
  all s: SystemState | s.mode = Normal =>
    all u: User |
      // Collect all roles assigned and delegated roles' roles of user u
      all r1, r2: u.assignedRoles + u.delegatedRoles.role |
        r1 != r2 implies no (r1.mutuallyExclusive & r2.mutuallyExclusive)
}

// Requests classification equals highest classification of affected objects
fact RequestClassification {
  all req: Request | 
    // If no objects, classification is Low
    (no req.objects => req.classification = Low) and
    // If some objects, classification is highest among them
    (some req.objects => {
      let cls = req.objects.classification | 
        (High in cls => req.classification = High) and
        ((High not in cls) and (Medium in cls) => req.classification = Medium) and
        ((High not in cls) and (Medium not in cls) => req.classification = Low)
    })
}

// Users can only submit requests with classification <= their clearance
fact RequestSubmissionClearance {
  all req: Request | clearanceAtLeast[req.submitter.clearance, req.classification]
}

// Requests are processed concurrently but in FIFO order in Normal mode
fact RequestFIFOProcessingNormalMode {
  all s: SystemState | s.mode = Normal =>
    all req1, req2: s.requests | 
      (req1.submittedAt < req2.submittedAt and req2.processedAt != none) implies
      (req1.processedAt != none and req1.processedAt < req2.processedAt)
}

// Delegated roles expire after fixed period or revoked anytime by admins
fact DelegatedRoleValidity {
  all dr: DelegatedRole | dr.revoked = False implies
    dr.expiryTime > dr.delegationTime
}

// === Prospective component requirements ===

// R1: Emergency Mode Triggering
// Exactly two administrators must simultaneously trigger emergency in Normal mode to enter Emergency mode
pred R1TriggerEmergency[s1, s2: SystemState] {
  s1.mode = Normal and s2.mode = Emergency and s1.time < s2.time and
  // Exactly two distinct administrators trigger emergency simultaneously at s2.time
  let triggers = { a: Administrator | TriggeredEmergency[a, s2.time] } |
  #triggers = 2 and
  all a: triggers | a in Administrator
}
// Abstract predicate for an administrator triggering emergency at a time
pred TriggeredEmergency[a: Administrator, t: Time] { some a } // placeholder for concurrency abstraction

// R2: Emergency Mode Duration and Recovery
fact R2EmergencyDuration {
  all s1, s2: SystemState | 
    (s1.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal and s1.time < s2.time) implies
    some t: Time | t > s1.time and s2.time = t
}

// R3: Emergency Mode Operations

// R3.1: Request processing may bypass FIFO to prioritize high-classification requests
fact R3_1_RequestProcessingEmergency {
  all s: SystemState | s.mode = Emergency =>
    // No strict FIFO order required; high-class requests may be processed concurrently
    no disj req1, req2: s.requests | 
      req1.submittedAt < req2.submittedAt and req2.processedAt != none and req1.processedAt != none and
      req1.processedAt > req2.processedAt and req1.classification = High and req2.classification != High
}

// R3.2: Mutual exclusion relaxed during Emergency mode
fact MutualExclusionRelaxedEmergency {
  all s: SystemState | s.mode = Emergency =>
    // No mutual exclusion enforced; trivially true, so no constraint needed
    true
}

// R3.3: Administrators temporarily hold emergency privileges during Emergency mode
fact R3_3_EmergencyPrivileges {
  all epg: EmergencyPrivilegeGrant | 
    epg.grantedAt in Time and
    // emergency privileges granted only during Emergency mode time window
    some s: SystemState | s.time = epg.grantedAt and s.mode = Emergency and
    // revoked at or before emergency end time
    (epg.revokedAt != none implies epg.revokedAt > epg.grantedAt)
}

// R3.4: Requests submitted exactly at Normal-to-Emergency boundary are held and processed by priority
fact R3_4_RequestHoldAndPriorityAtBoundary {
  // Requests submitted exactly at s2.time (Normal->Emergency boundary) are not processed at s1.time (Normal)
  all s1, s2: SystemState | s1.mode = Normal and s2.mode = Emergency and s1.time < s2.time =>
    all req: s1.requests | req.submittedAt = s2.time => req.processedAt = none

  // Once Emergency mode is active, requests at boundary processed in priority order:
  // all High first, then Medium, then Low
  all s: SystemState | s.mode = Emergency => (
    // For any two requests at s.time with submittedAt = s.time:
    all req1, req2: s.requests | req1.submittedAt = s.time and req2.submittedAt = s.time and req1 != req2 =>
      (clearanceAtLeast[req1.classification, req2.classification] => 
        // req1 processed before or concurrently with req2 (processedAt <= processedAt)
        req1.processedAt != none and req2.processedAt != none and req1.processedAt <= req2.processedAt)
  )
}

// R4: Post-Emergency Cleanup and Updates

// R4.1: Emergency privileges revoked when Emergency mode ends
fact R4_1_EmergencyPrivilegesRevokedAtEnd {
  all epg: EmergencyPrivilegeGrant | 
    some endTime: Time | 
      // endTime is the time when emergency mode ends
      ((some s1, s2: SystemState | s1.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal and s1.time < s2.time and endTime = s2.time) =>
      epg.revokedAt = endTime)
}

// R4.2: Roles delegated during Emergency mode revoked at emergency end; prior delegated roles continue
fact R4_2_DelegatedRolesRevokedAtEnd {
  all dr: DelegatedRole |
    // If delegated during Emergency mode, revoked at emergency end
    (some s1, s2: SystemState | s1.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal and
      s1.time <= dr.delegationTime and dr.delegationTime < s2.time) =>
      dr.revoked = True
}

// R4.3: Administrators must update credentials immediately after Emergency mode ends (soft constraint)
// Modeled as a fact but will be verified as a soft constraint via assertion
fact R4_3_CredentialUpdateRequired {
  all s1, s2: SystemState |
    s1.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal and s1.time < s2.time =>
      // At s2.time, credentialUpdated flag for all admins is True
      all a: Administrator | s2.credentialUpdated[a] = True
}

// === Predicates for scenarios and transitions ===

// Incremental predicates for stepwise verification

pred R1 {
  // Model initial Normal mode system state and simultaneous trigger by exactly two admins to Emergency mode
  some s1, s2: SystemState | 
    s1.mode = Normal and s2.mode = Emergency and s1.time < s2.time and
    R1TriggerEmergency[s1, s2]
}

pred R1R2 {
  R1 and
  // Emergency mode duration finite and recovery to Normal mode
  some s1, s2: SystemState | s1.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal and s1.time < s2.time and
    s2.time != s1.time  // abstract finite duration, time difference unspecified
}

pred R1R2R3 {
  R1R2 and
  // Emergency mode operational changes apply
  all s: SystemState | s.mode = Emergency => (
    // Request processing may bypass FIFO
    R3_1_RequestProcessingEmergency and
    // Mutual exclusion relaxed (documented; no constraint needed)
    true and
    // Emergency privileges granted
    some epg: EmergencyPrivilegeGrant | epg.grantedAt = s.time
  )
}

pred All_Requirements {
  R1R2R3 and
  // Post-emergency cleanup and credential updates enforced
  all s1, s2: SystemState | s1.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal and s1.time < s2.time => (
    R4_1_EmergencyPrivilegesRevokedAtEnd and
    R4_2_DelegatedRolesRevokedAtEnd and
    R4_3_CredentialUpdateRequired
  )
}

// === Assertions to verify key properties ===

// Assert exactly two admins trigger emergency simultaneously
assert assertR1_TriggerSimultaneity {
  all s1, s2: SystemState | s1.mode = Normal and s2.mode = Emergency and s1.time < s2.time =>
    let triggers = { a: Administrator | TriggeredEmergency[a, s2.time] } |
    #triggers = 2
}

// Assert emergency duration finite and system returns to Normal eventually
assert assertR2_EmergencyFiniteDuration {
  all s1: SystemState | s1.mode = Emergency =>
    some s2: SystemState | s2.mode = Normal and s2.time > s1.time
}

// Assert mutual exclusion enforced only outside Emergency mode
assert assertR3_MutualExclusionRelaxedOnlyEmergency {
  all s: SystemState | (
    s.mode = Emergency or
    (all u: User | all r1, r2: u.assignedRoles + u.delegatedRoles.role | 
      r1 != r2 implies no (r1.mutuallyExclusive & r2.mutuallyExclusive))
  )
}

// Assert post-emergency cleanup: emergency privileges revoked, delegated roles revoked, credentials updated
assert assertR4_PostEmergencyCleanup {
  all s1, s2: SystemState | s1.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal and s1.time < s2.time =>
    // Emergency privileges revoked
    all epg: EmergencyPrivilegeGrant | epg.revokedAt = s2.time and
    // Emergency delegated roles revoked
    all dr: DelegatedRole | (dr.delegationTime >= s1.time and dr.delegationTime < s2.time) implies dr.revoked = True and
    // Admins updated credentials (soft constraint)
    all a: Administrator | s2.credentialUpdated[a] = True
}

// Additional assertions to strengthen verification

// Assert that fewer than two admins cannot trigger emergency mode
assert assertR1_NoLessThanTwoAdminsTrigger {
  all s1, s2: SystemState | s1.mode = Normal and s2.mode = Emergency and s1.time < s2.time =>
    let triggers = { a: Administrator | TriggeredEmergency[a, s2.time] } |
    #triggers >= 2
}

// Assert delegated roles assigned before Emergency mode remain after Emergency ends
assert assertR4_DelegatedRolesBeforeRemain {
  all dr: DelegatedRole |
    (some s1, s2: SystemState | s1.mode = Emergency and s2.mode = Normal and s1.time < s2.time and dr.delegationTime < s1.time) =>
    dr.revoked = False
}

// Assert all requests are eventually processed (liveness) in Normal and Emergency modes
assert assertLiveness {
  all req: Request | some s: SystemState | req in s.requests and req.processedAt != none
}

// Assert requests submitted at Normal->Emergency boundary are held at Normal and processed by priority in Emergency
assert assertR3_4_BoundaryRequestHandling {
  all s1, s2: SystemState | s1.mode = Normal and s2.mode = Emergency and s1.time < s2.time =>
    all req: s1.requests | req.submittedAt = s2.time => req.processedAt = none
}

// === Run and check commands for verification ===

run R1 for 5 but 3 User, 3 Administrator, 5 Role, 5 SystemObject, 3 Request, 5 Time, 5 DelegatedRole, 3 EmergencyPrivilegeGrant
run R1R2 for 5 but 3 User, 3 Administrator, 5 Role, 5 SystemObject, 3 Request, 5 Time, 5 DelegatedRole, 3 EmergencyPrivilegeGrant
run R1R2R3 for 5 but 3 User, 3 Administrator, 5 Role, 5 SystemObject, 3 Request, 5 Time, 5 DelegatedRole, 3 EmergencyPrivilegeGrant
run All_Requirements for 6 but 4 User, 4 Administrator, 6 Role, 6 SystemObject, 4 Request, 6 Time, 6 DelegatedRole, 4 EmergencyPrivilegeGrant

check assertR1_TriggerSimultaneity for 5 but 3 User, 3 Administrator, 5 Role, 5 SystemObject, 3 Request, 5 Time, 5 DelegatedRole, 3 EmergencyPrivilegeGrant
check assertR2_EmergencyFiniteDuration for 5 but 3 User, 3 Administrator, 5 Role, 5 SystemObject, 3 Request, 5 Time, 5 DelegatedRole, 3 EmergencyPrivilegeGrant
check assertR3_MutualExclusionRelaxedOnlyEmergency for 5 but 3 User, 3 Administrator, 5 Role, 5 SystemObject, 3 Request, 5 Time, 5 DelegatedRole, 3 EmergencyPrivilegeGrant
check assertR4_PostEmergencyCleanup for 6 but 4 User, 4 Administrator, 6 Role, 6 SystemObject, 4 Request, 6 Time, 6 DelegatedRole, 4 EmergencyPrivilegeGrant
check assertR1_NoLessThanTwoAdminsTrigger for 5 but 3 User, 3 Administrator, 5 Role, 5 SystemObject, 3 Request, 5 Time, 5 DelegatedRole, 3 EmergencyPrivilegeGrant
check assertR4_DelegatedRolesBeforeRemain for 6 but 4 User, 4 Administrator, 6 Role, 6 SystemObject, 4 Request, 6 Time, 6 DelegatedRole, 4 EmergencyPrivilegeGrant
check assertLiveness for 6 but 4 User, 4 Administrator, 6 Role, 6 SystemObject, 4 Request, 6 Time, 6 DelegatedRole, 4 EmergencyPrivilegeGrant
check assertR3_4_BoundaryRequestHandling for 6 but 4 User, 4 Administrator, 6 Role, 6 SystemObject, 4 Request, 6 Time, 6 DelegatedRole, 4 EmergencyPrivilegeGrant
```

